# 22. Common RBAC Mistakes

## Mistake 1: Creating a Role but no RoleBinding

You create:

```text
Role
 |
 +-- pod-reader
```

But nobody is assigned to it.

The Role defines permissions, but it does not automatically give them to anyone.

You need:

```text
Subject
   |
   v
RoleBinding
   |
   v
Role
```

---

## Mistake 2: Wrong namespace

Suppose the ServiceAccount is:

```text
dev/app-sa
```

but the RoleBinding references:

```text
prod/app-sa
```

These are different ServiceAccounts.

Always verify:

```bash
kubectl get sa -A
```

---

## Mistake 3: Wrong API group

Pods:

```yaml
apiGroups: [""]
```

Deployments:

```yaml
apiGroups: ["apps"]
```

Jobs:

```yaml
apiGroups: ["batch"]
```

Using the wrong API group can cause an apparently correct RBAC rule to fail.

---

## Mistake 4: Missing verb

Suppose you grant:

```yaml
verbs:
  - get
```

Then:

```bash
kubectl get pod
```

may be allowed.

But:

```bash
kubectl delete pod
```

will not be allowed.

Remember:

```text
get != list != create != update != delete
```

---

## Mistake 5: Giving cluster-wide permissions unnecessarily

Avoid using:

```text
ClusterRoleBinding
```

when a namespace-scoped:

```text
Role + RoleBinding
```

is sufficient.

Always ask:

> Does this application really need cluster-wide access?

